  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved capacity of constituents to implement sector-specific productivity policies and programmes that advance decent work and contribute to inclusive labour market institutions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia faces persistent challenges in generating sufficient decent and productive employment, particularly for youth, women, refugees, and vulnerable groups. High levels of informality, skills mismatches, weak labour market systems, and low productivity in key sectors constrain access to quality jobs and inclusive growth.
CPO ETH127 addresses these challenges by strengthening the capacity of government and social partners to design and implement integrated employment, skills, and labour market policies. It promotes a comprehensive approach linking employment policy, skills development, enterprise support, and labour market governance. The programme supports the integration of employment objectives into national and sectoral policies, improves labour market services and programmes, enhances demand-driven skills systems, strengthens MSMEs, and advances opportunities in the digital economy.
These efforts lead to more coherent and inclusive employment policies, stronger alignment between skills supply and labour market demand, and more effective labour market institutions. Access to employment opportunities expands, supporting transitions from informal to decent work, while productivity and working conditions improve in priority sectors. At the same time, digital economy strategies contribute to new job opportunities and innovation, resulting in increased decent job creation, improved employability, and better livelihoods, particularly for youth, women, refugees, and host communities.
Government institutions, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, and service delivery systems benefit from strengthened capacities to design and implement policies and programmes. Jobseekers and workers&#x2014;especially young people, women, refugees, and vulnerable populations&#x2014;gain improved access to skills, employment services, and livelihoods, while enterprises and the broader economy benefit from a more skilled workforce, higher productivity, and more inclusive and resilient labour markets.</narrative>
  </description>
